WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Each comparison product has
two properties: odd order,

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.580
and residue one modulo one plus i.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.360
We will show that together they
force exact equality to one.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.260
Begin with the cube roots
one, omega, and omega squared.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.340
Their exact relation is one plus
omega plus omega squared equals zero.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.420
Suppose omega had residue
one. Its square would too.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.560
If instead omega squared had residue one,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.820
squaring it gives omega, again
of residue one. In either case,

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.700
reducing the exact relation would give
one plus one plus one equals zero.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.820
In characteristic two, a pair of ones
disappears and the third remains.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.300
That would say one equals zero,
impossible in a nonzero ring.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.780
The two nontrivial candidates are
excluded. Now take the general case.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.340
Let A be a characteristic-zero domain,

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.080
with a ring map into a nonzero
ring of characteristic two.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.100
Let alpha have odd
order r and residue one.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.980
Order one already means alpha equals one.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.180
Suppose instead that alpha is not
one, so r is greater than one.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.020
Work in the original domain. Factor the
difference between alpha to the r and one

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.580
as alpha minus one times the sum of
the first r powers, starting at one.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.540
The power alpha to the r is
one, so this product is zero.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.800
Since alpha minus one is
nonzero and A is a domain,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.640
the geometric sum itself is zero. This
cancellation takes place before reduction.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.300
Only now apply the ring map.
Every power of alpha becomes one.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.640
Write the odd integer r as two k plus
one. The sum has k pairs of ones,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.020
each equal to zero in characteristic
two, and one term left over.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.180
The exact vanishing sum would
therefore give zero equals one.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.240
This contradicts the target ring
being nonzero. Alpha must equal one.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.260
The target ring need not be a domain; we
cancelled only in the original domain.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.200
Apply the lemma separately to R,
Delta of d divided by Delta of c,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.220
and to W, Delta of g
divided by Delta of c.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.860
They belong to A, since their
evaluated factors are units there.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.080
Node six point one gives odd
order. Node six point three gives

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.685
residue one in the nonzero quotient
established in Node six point two.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.900
Combining those two inputs yields
the exact identities R equals

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.980
one and W equals one. These
are arithmetic conclusions,

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.700
with no estimate of complex distance.
We can now unpack the comparison

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.900
products while keeping the original
evaluation points distinct.
